---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyAffordability from '@/components/tools/finance/CalcMoneyAffordability.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="house-affordability-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyAffordability client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How a lender decides what you can borrow</h2>
    <p>
      A lender takes your gross monthly income and applies two limits to it. The front-end ratio caps
      the housing payment alone. The back-end ratio caps the housing payment plus all your other debt
      payments. The lower of the two is your limit, and this house affordability calculator shows which
      one it is.
    </p>
    <p>
      The conventional benchmark is 28/36. FHA underwriting is looser, around 31/43. The Qualified
      Mortgage rule caps the back-end ratio at 43% for most loans that lenders want to keep saleable.
      The comparison table above applies each of these to your income.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the housing figure is PITI, not the mortgage payment</h2>
    <p>
      Lenders test the full cost of the home, not only the loan:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Principal and interest: the mortgage payment.</li>
      <li>
        Taxes: property tax, usually a percentage of assessed value, collected monthly into escrow.
      </li>
      <li>Insurance: homeowners cover, required for the life of the loan.</li>
      <li>
        PMI: private mortgage insurance, charged while the loan is more than 80% of value. It protects
        the lender, not you.
      </li>
      <li>
        HOA or condo fees: not part of the mortgage, but counted in full because you pay them each
        month.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This matters most in high-tax areas. A 1.1% property tax rate on a 400,000 house is 367 a month.
      That is more than a fifth of the total housing payment, and it moves your maximum price by tens of
      thousands.
    </p>

    <h2>What existing debt really costs you</h2>
    <p>
      Car finance and student loans reduce your limit through the back-end ratio. Each 100 of monthly
      debt payment removes 100 of housing allowance. At a 6.25% thirty-year rate, with property tax,
      insurance and PMI counted, that is about 15,000 of purchase price.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Monthly debt payments</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Housing allowance left (36% back-end)</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Approx. price impact</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>0</td><td style="text-align:right">full 28% front-end applies</td><td style="text-align:right">0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>200</td><td style="text-align:right">−200</td><td style="text-align:right">−30,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>400</td><td style="text-align:right">−400</td><td style="text-align:right">−60,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>700</td><td style="text-align:right">−700</td><td style="text-align:right">−105,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1,000</td><td style="text-align:right">−1,000</td><td style="text-align:right">−150,000</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm">
      Rough figures at 6.25% over 30 years with a 10% deposit and typical tax, insurance and PMI. To pay
      off a car loan before you apply often increases your budget more than any other step.
    </p>

    <h2>Lenders count minimum payments, not balances</h2>
    <p>
      A 12,000 card balance with a 240 minimum counts as 240, not 12,000. This has two results. If you
      pay a card down and keep it open, the minimum falls and your ratio improves. If you consolidate
      short-term debt into a longer term, the monthly payment falls and the ratio improves, but the total
      owed stays the same and you pay more interest overall.
    </p>
    <p>
      Lenders do not count utilities, groceries, childcare, commuting, phone bills, pension
      contributions, or other costs that are not credit obligations. So a lender's maximum is a ceiling,
      not a recommendation.
    </p>

    <h2>How the maximum price is solved</h2>
    <p>
      There is no closed-form answer, because property tax and PMI both depend on the price. A higher
      price means a larger loan, which raises the interest, the tax and the PMI together. So the
      calculator uses bisection. It halves the search interval between the down payment and a very large
      number, again and again, and tests if the PITI fits inside both caps. After sixty iterations the
      answer is accurate to well under one unit of currency. The working panel shows the test at the
      final price.
    </p>

    <h2>What this does not include</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Closing costs: usually 2% to 5% of the price in the US, plus stamp duty or land transfer tax
        elsewhere. You pay these from your cash, not the loan.
      </li>
      <li>
        Reserves: many lenders want several months of PITI left in the bank after closing.
      </li>
      <li>
        Maintenance: a common rule of thumb is 1% of the property value a year, and you must pay it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Rate movement: a one-point rise in rates cuts buying power by about 10%. Run the numbers again
        when you get your rate lock quote.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why this page has no form</h2>
    <p>
      This page has no lead form and no account. Your income and debts stay in this browser tab, and the
      page also works offline.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
